{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "treatment-planning",
    "title": "Treatment Planning",
    "order": 13
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "treatment-planning-pelvis",
    "title": "Pelvis Treatment Planning",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "One Site, Several Disease Types",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The pelvis hosts several distinct disease types under one ARRT category: prostate, cervical, uterine, rectal, and anal cancers. The planning mechanics share enough in common \u2014 positioning, OARs, bladder/rectum management \u2014 to group them together, even though the actual target volumes differ by diagnosis."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Supine vs. Prone Positioning",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Position"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Rationale"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Supine",
                                  "marks": [
                                    {
                                      "type": "bold"
                                    }
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Most common; comfortable, highly reproducible, compatible with knee/ankle immobilization devices"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Prone (with belly board)",
                                  "marks": [
                                    {
                                      "type": "bold"
                                    }
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Used for some rectal/anal treatments \u2014 a belly board opening lets the small bowel fall anteriorly, away from the treatment field"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page2_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "What is the main reason a belly board is used for some rectal treatments?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "It moves the small bowel out of the treatment field",
              "It improves patient comfort only"
            ],
            "answer": 0,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The belly board opening lets small bowel loops fall away from the field anteriorly, directly sparing bowel dose \u2014 not just a comfort measure."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Bladder and Rectum Protocols",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Both bladder and rectal "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "filling state",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " directly affect where pelvic organs sit day to day \u2014 this is managed deliberately, not left to chance:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "A "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "full-bladder protocol",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " (patient drinks a set amount of water before simulation and every treatment) pushes small bowel superiorly, out of the field, and creates a more reproducible target position. Rectal protocols (empty rectum, sometimes with an enema beforehand) similarly aim for day-to-day consistency."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Target Volumes",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Because \"Pelvis\" spans several diagnoses, the GTV/CTV/PTV definitions look genuinely different depending on the disease:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Diagnosis"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "CTV typically includes"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Prostate"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Prostate \u00b1 seminal vesicles; pelvic nodes if intermediate/high risk"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Cervical / Uterine"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Uterus/cervix, parametria, upper vagina, and pelvic \u00b1 para-aortic nodes depending on stage"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Rectal / Anal"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Primary tumor/mesorectum plus the draining pelvic (and inguinal, for anal) nodal regions"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "PTV margins in the pelvis are chosen specifically with the bladder/rectum filling protocols from the previous page in mind \u2014 tighter margins are only safe when day-to-day organ filling is well controlled and verified with image guidance."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page4_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Why does the pelvis need larger or smaller PTV margins depending on bladder/rectum protocol adherence?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Because inconsistent organ filling directly increases day-to-day target position uncertainty",
              "Margins in the pelvis are fixed regardless of protocol adherence"
            ],
            "answer": 0,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Bladder and rectal filling directly shift pelvic organ position \u2014 the more consistent the filling, the more confidently a tighter margin can be used."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Organ-at-Risk Constraints",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Structure"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Constraint (conventional prostate example)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Bladder"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "V70 \u2264 25\u201335% (volume receiving 70 Gy)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Rectum"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "V70 \u2264 20% (volume receiving 70 Gy)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Femoral heads"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "V50 \u2264 5%"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "Bowel (gyn/rectal cases)"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": [
                                {
                                  "type": "text",
                                  "text": "V45 \u2264 195 cc"
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "For a standard prostate case specifically, the two organs at risk you'll hear referenced constantly are the "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "bladder and rectum",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " \u2014 both directly adjacent to the prostate on essentially every side."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page5_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "What are the two primary organs at risk in a standard prostate treatment?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Bladder and rectum",
              "Liver and kidneys"
            ],
            "answer": 0,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The bladder and rectum sit immediately adjacent to the prostate and are the two OARs most consistently discussed in prostate planning."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Techniques",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "VMAT/IMRT",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " is standard for definitive pelvic treatment, allowing dose to conform tightly around bladder/rectum. "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Image guidance",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " (daily CBCT, or fiducial-based kV imaging for prostate) is essential given how much bladder/rectal filling can shift the target day to day. "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Brachytherapy",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " plays a major role specifically in cervical and some prostate cases, often combined with external beam."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Wrap-Up",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You've covered supine vs. prone positioning, bladder/rectum filling protocols, diagnosis-specific target volumes, OAR constraints, and technique selection across the pelvic disease types."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Cross-reference: this lesson covers the planning mechanics common across prostate, cervical, uterine, rectal, and anal disease \u2014 for disease-specific anatomy and staging, see the corresponding pages under Tumor Sites Localization (Genitourinary, Male/Female Reproductive, and GI Tumors)."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
